WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.179
A family tree, the folders on your disk

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:05.842
the chapters and sections of a book

00:00:05.842 --> 00:00:08.505
the classification of living things.

00:00:08.505 --> 00:00:10.592
All of them are hierarchies.

00:00:10.592 --> 00:00:12.535
One thing sits at the top

00:00:12.607 --> 00:00:14.838
and everything else hangs below

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:16.853
exactly one thing above it.

00:00:16.925 --> 00:00:19.085
Here is a small classification

00:00:19.156 --> 00:00:20.380
of living things.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.186
A tree is the data structure

00:00:23.274 --> 00:00:24.677
for hierarchies.

00:00:24.677 --> 00:00:28.273
Arrays, lists, stacks and queues put the

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:30.027
elements in a line.

00:00:30.115 --> 00:00:32.483
A tree lets an element have

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:34.237
several successors.

00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:37.570
It is our first non-linear structure.

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.483
The definition is recursive.

00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:42.483
A tree is either empty

00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:44.317
with no nodes at all

00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:47.483
or it has one special node, the root

00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:51.233
here Organism, and the other nodes are split

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.567
into zero or more subtrees

00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:56.233
each of which is itself a tree.

00:00:56.317 --> 00:00:59.150
The root of each subtree is joined

00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:01.150
to the root by an edge.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.886
Animal's own subtrees are Mammal and Fish

00:01:04.886 --> 00:01:07.144
and so on all the way down.

00:01:07.144 --> 00:01:09.643
Note that a tree may be empty.

00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:12.626
For binary trees this matters a lot

00:01:12.706 --> 00:01:15.125
because a node's left or right

00:01:15.206 --> 00:01:17.060
subtree is often empty.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.786
Two facts follow straight

00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:21.588
from the definition.

00:01:21.588 --> 00:01:22.188
First

00:01:22.188 --> 00:01:25.621
every node is the root of some subtree

00:01:25.621 --> 00:01:28.453
Animal with everything below it

00:01:28.453 --> 00:01:31.543
and even a single leaf like Mammal.

00:01:31.628 --> 00:01:34.546
That is why almost every algorithm

00:01:34.632 --> 00:01:36.520
on trees is recursive.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.703
Second, a tree with N nodes has exactly

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:42.570
N − 1 edges.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:45.911
Count each edge at its lower end

00:01:45.911 --> 00:01:49.645
one edge into Plant, one into Animal

00:01:49.645 --> 00:01:52.790
one into Mammal, one into Fish.

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.728
The root has no edge coming in.

00:01:55.728 --> 00:01:57.982
So five nodes, four edges

00:01:58.065 --> 00:02:00.904
exactly what the program TreeTerms

00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:02.740
prints for this tree.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.682
Trees borrow the words of family trees.

00:02:06.682 --> 00:02:09.786
When an edge goes from A down to B

00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:13.665
A is the parent of B, and B is a child of A.

00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:17.200
Animal is the parent of Mammal and Fish.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:21.253
Nodes with the same parent are siblings

00:02:21.253 --> 00:02:23.290
like Mammal and Fish.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:27.178
A node with no children is a leaf: Plant

00:02:27.178 --> 00:02:28.752
Mammal and Fish.

00:02:28.844 --> 00:02:31.622
A node with at least one child

00:02:31.714 --> 00:02:35.510
is an internal node: Organism and Animal.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.467
Going up again and again gives

00:02:38.554 --> 00:02:40.205
a node's ancestors

00:02:40.205 --> 00:02:44.029
Fish has the ancestors Animal and Organism.

00:02:44.029 --> 00:02:46.984
Going down gives its descendants

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.069
Mammal and Fish are the

00:02:49.156 --> 00:02:51.068
descendants of Animal.

00:02:51.155 --> 00:02:53.414
Every node except the root

00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:55.500
has exactly one parent.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.424
A path is a sequence of nodes in which

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:02.680
each node is the parent of the next.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.760
From Organism, to Animal, to Fish.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:08.139
The length of a path is the

00:03:08.214 --> 00:03:09.868
number of edges on it

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:11.746
not the number of nodes.

00:03:11.746 --> 00:03:13.550
Three nodes, two edges

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.504
this path has length 2.

00:03:15.504 --> 00:03:18.510
A single node is a path of length 0.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.466
And since every node has only one parent

00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:25.807
there is only one way up to the root.

00:03:25.895 --> 00:03:28.358
So there is exactly one path

00:03:28.446 --> 00:03:30.820
from the root to each node.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.097
Depth and height are easy to mix up.

00:03:34.097 --> 00:03:36.494
Depth is measured from the top

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:39.266
the depth of a node is the length of

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.887
the path from the root down to it.

00:03:41.887 --> 00:03:43.760
The root has depth 0

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.382
its children depth 1, and so on

00:03:46.382 --> 00:03:47.880
down to depth 4.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:51.635
Height is measured from the bottom

00:03:51.732 --> 00:03:55.721
the height of a node is the length of the

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:59.321
longest path from it down to a leaf.

00:03:59.321 --> 00:04:02.045
Every leaf has height 0.

00:04:02.045 --> 00:04:05.450
C has depth 1, and height 3

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.493
the path C, F, G, H.

00:04:07.493 --> 00:04:11.190
B also has depth 1, but height 1.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.644
The height of a tree is the

00:04:13.722 --> 00:04:16.060
height of its root: 4 here

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.787
and that equals the largest depth.

00:04:18.865 --> 00:04:21.047
By convention the empty tree

00:04:21.124 --> 00:04:22.761
has height -1

00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:26.579
so that height equals 1 plus the larger height

00:04:26.657 --> 00:04:29.540
of the subtrees works for leaves too.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.113
Now predict.

00:04:31.113 --> 00:04:32.271
In this tree

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:35.580
what are the depth and the height of F?

00:04:35.662 --> 00:04:37.813
And which nodes have depth

00:04:37.896 --> 00:04:39.550
two and height zero?

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.280
Pause the video and work it out.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:49.475
F has depth two: the path A, C,

00:04:49.475 --> 00:04:51.457
F has two edges.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:54.256
Its height is also two

00:04:54.256 --> 00:04:58.220
the longest path down is F, G, H.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:02.110
D and E have depth two and height zero

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.287
they are leaves one level below B.

00:05:05.378 --> 00:05:09.191
The program TreeTerms builds this tree and

00:05:09.282 --> 00:05:12.640
prints the vocabulary for every node

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.547
and its lines for F

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.907
D and E say exactly that.

00:05:16.907 --> 00:05:20.810
H and I are leaves too, but at depth four.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.045
In Java

00:05:22.045 --> 00:05:25.237
a general tree node keeps its element

00:05:25.237 --> 00:05:27.446
a reference to its parent

00:05:27.446 --> 00:05:29.738
and a list of its children.

00:05:29.738 --> 00:05:32.603
Depth walks up: if parent is null

00:05:32.603 --> 00:05:35.058
this is the root, depth zero.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.513
Otherwise it is one more than

00:05:37.595 --> 00:05:39.150
the parent's depth.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.478
Height recurses down.

00:05:41.478 --> 00:05:43.268
It starts at zero

00:05:43.268 --> 00:05:45.811
which is right for a leaf.

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:47.601
For every child c

00:05:47.695 --> 00:05:50.332
it takes the larger of h and

00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:52.310
one plus c's height.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.100
Then it returns h.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:55.261
A warning.

00:05:55.261 --> 00:05:58.584
Depth and height count edges, not nodes.

00:05:58.584 --> 00:06:00.610
The root has depth zero

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.880
and a leaf has height zero.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.798
Some books and exam questions count

00:06:05.879 --> 00:06:07.824
levels from one instead

00:06:07.824 --> 00:06:10.094
with the root on level one.

00:06:10.094 --> 00:06:13.255
Whenever you read a height or a depth

00:06:13.255 --> 00:06:15.930
check which convention is meant.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:19.880
From now on we focus on one kind of tree.

00:06:19.962 --> 00:06:22.838
A binary tree is a tree in which no

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.550
node has more than two children.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.425
The two possible subtrees of a node are called

00:06:29.502 --> 00:06:32.491
its left subtree and its right subtree

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.020
and either or both may be empty.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.227
Left and right matter.

00:06:37.311 --> 00:06:39.843
Here are two binary trees with

00:06:39.927 --> 00:06:42.290
root A and one more node, B.

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:45.243
In the first, B is the left child.

00:06:45.243 --> 00:06:48.450
In the second, it is the right child.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.991
As general trees they would be the same.

00:06:51.991 --> 00:06:54.864
As binary trees they are different.

00:06:54.864 --> 00:06:57.576
So a binary tree is either empty

00:06:57.576 --> 00:07:00.130
or a root with an ordered pair

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:03.640
a left binary tree and a right binary tree.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.134
Two shapes of binary tree

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.047
come up again and again.

00:07:08.047 --> 00:07:09.655
Here are four trees

00:07:09.655 --> 00:07:11.645
numbered as on the slide.

00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:14.324
A tree is full when every node has

00:07:14.401 --> 00:07:16.544
either 0 or 2 children

00:07:16.544 --> 00:07:18.458
no node has exactly 1.

00:07:18.534 --> 00:07:20.754
A tree is complete when every

00:07:20.831 --> 00:07:22.898
level is completely filled

00:07:22.898 --> 00:07:25.347
except possibly the lowest one

00:07:25.424 --> 00:07:27.184
and the lowest level is

00:07:27.261 --> 00:07:29.940
filled from the left, without gaps.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.677
The first tree is not full

00:07:32.677 --> 00:07:35.417
node 3 has only 1 child.

00:07:35.417 --> 00:07:37.538
And it is not complete

00:07:37.538 --> 00:07:41.251
3 has no left child, but 5 exists

00:07:41.251 --> 00:07:42.930
so there is a gap.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.808
The second is full

00:07:44.808 --> 00:07:48.184
but its lowest level starts on the right

00:07:48.184 --> 00:07:50.033
so it is not complete.

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:51.882
The third is complete

00:07:51.882 --> 00:07:55.258
but 2 has 1 child, so it is not full.

00:07:55.258 --> 00:07:56.866
The fourth is both.

00:07:56.866 --> 00:07:59.760
The two properties are independent.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.202
A tree whose leaves all sit at

00:08:02.272 --> 00:08:04.085
the same depth is perfect

00:08:04.085 --> 00:08:05.550
every level is full.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.711
Books disagree on these names

00:08:07.711 --> 00:08:10.710
so always check the definition being used.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.586
How many nodes fit in a binary tree?

00:08:14.586 --> 00:08:17.882
Each node has at most 2 children

00:08:17.974 --> 00:08:21.727
so the number of nodes can at most double

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:24.291
from one depth to the next.

00:08:24.291 --> 00:08:28.228
1 at depth 0, 2 at depth 1, 4

00:08:28.228 --> 00:08:31.890
8: at most 2^d at depth d.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.968
Add them up: a tree of height h has at most

00:08:36.055 --> 00:08:39.436
2^(h+1) − 1 nodes.

00:08:39.436 --> 00:08:41.864
Height 3 gives 15

00:08:41.864 --> 00:08:44.812
exactly when the tree is perfect.

00:08:44.812 --> 00:08:47.500
And at least h + 1 nodes

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:48.800
a single path.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:50.169
Turned round

00:08:50.248 --> 00:08:53.149
a binary tree with n nodes has height

00:08:53.227 --> 00:08:55.971
at least the floor of log₂ n

00:08:55.971 --> 00:08:58.480
which a complete tree achieves

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.929
and at most n − 1, for a skewed tree

00:09:01.929 --> 00:09:04.830
where every node has only one child.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.153
One more fact: in a full binary tree

00:09:08.233 --> 00:09:11.206
there is always exactly one more leaf

00:09:11.286 --> 00:09:13.697
than there are internal nodes.

00:09:13.777 --> 00:09:16.187
Many tree operations take time

00:09:16.268 --> 00:09:18.437
proportional to the height

00:09:18.517 --> 00:09:21.249
so keeping trees short will matter

00:09:21.329 --> 00:09:23.820
a great deal in later lectures.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:25.281
To sum up.

00:09:25.281 --> 00:09:27.220
One: a tree is empty

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.424
or a root with subtrees.

00:09:29.424 --> 00:09:32.244
N nodes have N − 1 edges

00:09:32.332 --> 00:09:34.888
and there is exactly one path

00:09:34.976 --> 00:09:37.356
from the root to each node.

00:09:37.356 --> 00:09:41.233
Two: depth counts edges down from the root

00:09:41.322 --> 00:09:43.613
and height counts edges up

00:09:43.701 --> 00:09:45.640
from the deepest leaf.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:49.080
Three: a binary tree has a left

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.173
and a right subtree.

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:51.771
Full

00:09:51.771 --> 00:09:55.858
complete and perfect describe its shape

00:09:55.957 --> 00:09:59.446
and a binary tree of height h holds

00:09:59.546 --> 00:10:02.536
at most 2^(h+1) − 1

00:10:02.536 --> 00:10:04.230
nodes.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:05.348
Try this.

00:10:05.348 --> 00:10:08.843
A complete binary tree has 20 nodes.

00:10:08.843 --> 00:10:10.549
What is its height?

00:10:10.549 --> 00:10:14.300
And how many nodes are on its lowest level?

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:15.559
In part two

00:10:15.559 --> 00:10:18.739
the binary tree as an abstract data type

00:10:18.739 --> 00:10:20.633
built from linked nodes

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:22.450
and stored in an array.

